.\" UNIX V7 source code: see /COPYRIGHT or www.tuhs.org for details.
.TH FOPEN 3S
.SH NAME
fopen, freopen, fdopen, open_memstream, tmpfile, tmpnam, remove, rename \- open,
name and remove a stream's file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.B FILE *fopen(const char *filename, const char *type);
.PP
.B FILE *freopen(const char *filename, const char *type, FILE *stream);
.PP
.B FILE *fdopen(int fildes, const char *type);
.PP
.B FILE *open_memstream(char **bufp, size_t *sizep);
.PP
.B FILE *tmpfile(void);
.PP
.B char *tmpnam(char *s);
.PP
.B int remove(const char *filename);
.PP
.B int rename(const char *from, const char *to);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Fopen
opens the file named by
.I filename
and associates a stream with it.
.I Fopen
returns a pointer to be used to identify
the stream in subsequent operations.
.PP
.I Type
is a character string having one of the following values:
.TP 5
"r"
open for reading
.ns
.TP 5
"w"
create for writing
.ns
.TP 5
"a"
append: open for writing at end
of file, or create for writing
.PP
.B Note:
a `+' may follow any of the three, opening the stream for update.
.B "w+"
and
.B "a+"
are created and then reopened, because
.IR creat (2)
leaves the descriptor write-only.  A `b' is accepted and ignored: there are no
text and binary streams here, only bytes.
.PP
.I Freopen
substitutes the named file in place
of the open
.IR stream .
It returns the original value of
.IR stream .
The original stream is closed.
.PP
.I Freopen
is typically used to attach the preopened
constant names,
.B stdin, stdout, stderr,
to specified files.
.PP
.I Fdopen
associates a stream with a file descriptor obtained from
.I open, dup, creat,
or
.IR pipe (2).
The
.I type
of the stream must agree with the mode of the open file.
.PP
.I Open_memstream
opens a stream for writing whose sink is not a file but a buffer on the heap that
grows to hold whatever is written to it.  It is POSIX.1\-2008 and was in neither
C11 nor v7.  The buffer and its length are reported through
.I bufp
and
.IR sizep ,
which POSIX says are valid after each
.IR fflush (3)
or
.IR fclose (3);
here they are current at every moment, because the stream is arranged so that the
library sees each byte as it is written.  The buffer is obtained from
.IR malloc (3)
and belongs to the caller, who must
.I free
it once the stream is closed.
.PP
The length in
.RI * sizep
excludes the null byte that always terminates the buffer, so the result may be read
either as a string or as
.RI * sizep
bytes.  A null byte written to the stream is data and is counted like any other.
.PP
.B Note:
what this port does not honour.  A memory stream carries no descriptor, so
.IR fseek (3),
.I rewind
and
.IR ungetc (3)
are not supported on one, and neither are
.IR setbuf (3)
and
.I setvbuf,
which would replace the very buffer the caller has been handed.
.IR Ftell (3)
.I is
supported and answers the number of bytes written.
.IR Fileno (3)
answers a number deliberately chosen to be no descriptor at all, so that a stray
.IR write (2)
or
.IR lseek (2)
on a memory stream fails with EBADF rather than reaching a real file.
.PP
.B Note:
an allocation failure while the buffer is growing
.I loses
the text.  This
.IR realloc (3)
frees a block before it allocates the new one and does not give it back on failure,
so there is nothing to fall back on; the loss is reported honestly as a null
.RI * bufp ,
a zero
.RI * sizep
and the error indication that
.IR ferror (3)
reads.
.I Free
accepts the null, so a caller that frees unconditionally is still correct.
.PP
.B Note:
the four that follow are C11 and had no v7 ancestor.
.PP
.I Tmpfile
creates a temporary file open for update and returns a stream on it.  The name is
.I unlinked
as soon as the stream holds the descriptor, so the file has no name from then on
and the kernel frees the inode when the last reference goes \- which is either
.IR fclose (3)
or the cleanup that
.IR exit (3)
runs.  Nothing is left behind even if the program dies on a signal.
.PP
.I Tmpnam
generates a file name that no existing file has, in a buffer of at least
.B L_tmpnam
characters; a null
.I s
asks for a static buffer that the next call overwrites.  It can distinguish
.B TMP_MAX
names.
.PP
.B Note:
.I tmpnam
does
.I not
go through
.IR mktemp (3).
That routine fills its trailing X's from the process id and then walks a
.I single
letter, so it can distinguish 26 names where TMP_MAX promises 26**3.
.I Tmpnam
counts three letters of its own, and the pid still separates one process's names
from another's.
.PP
.I Remove
deletes a file;
.I rename
changes its name.  Both return 0 on success and a non-zero value on failure, and
both are thin wrappers over
.IR unlink (2)
and
.IR link (2)/ unlink (2).
.SH "SEE ALSO"
open(2),
fclose(3),
setbuf(3),
mktemp(3),
stdio(3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Fopen,
.I freopen,
.I fdopen
and
.I tmpfile
return the pointer
.SM
.B NULL
if
.I filename
cannot be accessed.
.I Open_memstream
returns NULL when every one of the FOPEN_MAX streams is already in use, or when
the initial buffer cannot be allocated.
.I Tmpnam
returns NULL if it cannot find an unused name.
.SH BUGS
.I Fdopen
is not portable to systems other than UNIX.
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
FOPEN_MAX is _NFILE, which is also NOFILE in <sys/param.h>: one
.B FILE
per descriptor and no more.
.PP
.B There is no path-length limit in this kernel.
.IR Namei ()
walks a component at a time and only the component is bounded, by DIRSIZ.  So
FILENAME_MAX is a convention for sizing a caller's buffer and not a kernel
number; it is v7's customary 128.
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libc.a
.br
lib/libc/stdio/fopen.c, freopen.c, fdopen.c, endopen.c, findiop.c, memstream.c,
tmpfile.c, tmpnam.c, remove.c, rename.c
